   "title": "Poland says Pentagon backs permanent US base, with Trump decision due in December",
   "url": "https://www.politico.eu/article/poland-says-pentagon-backs-permanent-us-base-with-trump-decision-due-in-december",
   "domain": "politico.eu",
   "source": "politico.eu",
   "published": "Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:18:01 GMT",
   "_dt": 1794341881.0,
   "ageDays": 0.41,
   "summary": "Poland's defense minister said Tuesday that the Pentagon had given a “green light” to plans for a permanent U.S. military base in the country, with President Donald Trump expected to make a final decision in early December. The Pentagon’s own account stopped short of endorsing the base or confirming the December timetable, but acknowledged ongoing talks about strengthening the permanent U.S. military presence in Poland.",
   "summary_zh": "波兰国防部长周二表示，五角大楼已为在该国永久设立美国军事基地的计划“开绿灯”，预计美国总统唐纳德·特朗普将于12月初作出最终决定。五角大楼自身的说法并未明确支持设立该基地，也未确认12月的时间安排，但承认双方正在就加强美国在波兰的永久军事存在进行磋商。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Poland's defense minister said Tuesday that the Pentagon had given a “green light” to plans for a permanent U.S. military base in the country, with President Donald Trump expected to make a final decision in early December. Speaking after talks with U.S. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth in Washington, Władysław Kosiniak-Kamysz said his American counterpart told him that “everything is moving in the right direction.” “We interpret that as a green light from the Pentagon,” he said. The project has not yet received formal U.S. approval. The Pentagon’s own account stopped short of endorsing the base or confirming the December timetable, but acknowledged ongoing talks about strengthening the permanent U.S. military presence in Poland. Hegseth praised Poland’s military buildup, calling its army “one of the most formidable” in Europe. He also pushed for European NATO allies to take greater responsibility for their own defense, potentially allowing Washington to reduce its troop presence on the continent. Poland already hosts thousands of American troops, largely on rotating deployments. But Warsaw is pushing for a permanent base as the Pentagon reviews its military presence across Europe, with other NATO allies fearing Trump could withdraw thousands more troops . The review is expected to conclude by December. The project is also a rare point of agreement between Poland's feuding leaders. Prime Minister Donald Tusk and President Karol Nawrocki — a Trump ally whom the U.S. president backed in last year's election — agreed last week to cooperate on securing the base, despite their bitter confrontation over control of Poland's top court. Nawrocki has embraced the proposed name “Fort Trump,” first floated by his predecessor, Andrzej Duda, in 2018. The U.S. Department of Defense didn’t immediately respond to a request for comment",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
