   "_dt": 1794322022.0,
   "ageDays": 0.64,
   "summary": "The Korean won climbed against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, as global oil prices declined with U.S. President Donald Trump signaling a possible resolution to the war with Iran. The won was quoted at 1,337.5 won per dollar at 3:30 p.m., up 9.6 won from the close of the previous stock trading session.",
   "summary_zh": "周二，随着全球油价下跌，且美国总统唐纳德·特朗普暗示与伊朗的战争可能得到解决，韩元兑美元汇率走高。下午3时30分，美元兑韩元报1,337.5韩元，较上一股市交易日收盘时上涨9.6韩元。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Korean won climbed against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, as global oil prices declined with U.S. President Donald Trump signaling a possible resolution to the war with Iran. The won was quoted at 1,337.5 won per dollar at 3:30 p.m., up 9.6 won from the close of the previous stock trading session. After meeting with Qatar Prime Minister Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman Al Thani, a key mediator between Washington and Tehran, at the White House, Trump told reporters that further strikes against Iran will not be necessary for the time being. The won's advance also got a boost from a sharp rebound in the stock market, with foreign investors turning to net buyers of local stocks. Foreign investors bought a net 350 billion won ($260 million) worth of local shares during the latest regular stock trading session, with the benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index jumping 4.39 percent to close at 5,239.13",
